    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d606">

                <pb facs="0659" n="561" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg561"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5462: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-08-01T18:00:00+08:00">August 1, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 1—6:03 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>882. Your 230, July 29, 6 p.m.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d606-f1"/> Question, if it
                    should arise, will be very difficult for us to handle. I anticipate that the
                    diplomatic body may act as a whole to endeavor to persuade Japanese to retire or
                    to be interned. Japanese attitude in the past, however, has been that they are
                    acting to protect lives and property and I anticipate that they will refuse to
                    retire or be interned. Should the question arise I shall here urge my colleagues
                    to recommend joint action at Tokyo and we will do what we can to persuade the
                    Japanese to leave protection of their people and their Legation to us.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
